Hi Jose,

I have an e-mail answering to this thread on the 24.03.2014, maybe you 
missed it or did I write your e-mail wrong?

http://permalink.gmane.org/gmane.science.biology.informatics.conductor/53937

Your concern is answered by the second point that I describe there. If you
look at your "fitted splicing" plot, you can see this.  The extreme case 
is the coefficients fitted
for your exon E032, it has a value of ~40,000 in one of your conditions 
and a value of ~800 on
your other conditions.  This will affect the estimation of relative exon 
abundances from
your other exons. As I mentioned before, this is a limitation of the 
DEXSeq model,
but luckily, genes like this cases seem to be exceptions rather than the 
rule
(at least in my experience!).

About using the output from voom to test for DEU, I have not explored 
that option,
but maybe the maintainers/authors of that package are able to guide you 
better.

Hope it is useful,
Alejandro




